{
  "text": "Six AI agents now do real work for you, even while you sleep. Same promise. Very different deals. Four of them live in a big company's cloud, on their own computer. Two are free, open source, and run on yours. First, OpenAI's Dots. Each dot gets its own cloud computer, links to over four thousand apps, and you can even text it on iMessage. It asks before it pays. It comes with ChatGPT Pro, from a hundred dollars a month, and it's not in Europe yet. Meta's Muse is the everyday helper. It books trips, shops, even negotiates your bills, right inside WhatsApp, and checks with you before it spends. Most tasks are free, with twenty and hundred dollar plans. For now, it's only in the US and Singapore. xAI's Grok Bot is the team player. Every bot gets its own computer, signs into your work apps, and joins your Slack, even your Zoom calls. It comes with X Premium at thirty dollars and up, and heavy use costs extra. Anthropic's Claude Duet is the careful one. It briefs you every morning, spots unpaid invoices, even runs payroll. Before it sends, pays, signs or deletes, it shows you and waits, and it never moves money between accounts. It's included in Claude Pro at twenty dollars, but it's a US waitlist preview. Now the free ones. OpenClaw, the lobster, has three hundred ninety-five thousand GitHub stars. It runs on your own computer, you chat with it on WhatsApp or Telegram, and the software costs nothing. But it can run commands on your machine, so lock it down. Hermes, from Nous Research, is the learner. After a hard task, it writes itself a new skill, so it gets better the more you use it. It's free with any AI model, or hosted from twenty dollars a month. The catch: you pick and set up the model yourself. So which one? Busy pro with budget: Dots. Everyday errands: Muse. A whole team: Grok Bot. Approval on every risky step: Claude Duet. Free and on your own machine: OpenClaw or Hermes. Save this before you pay for an agent. Which one would you trust with your inbox? Tell me below.",
